---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ConsultLine from '../components/ConsultLine.astro';

const updated = 'August 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Terms | The MailDoc"
  description="The tools are free to use, for any domain you are allowed to check. What we promise about accuracy, and what we do not."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="md-wrap md-prose">
      <p class="md-eyebrow" style="color: var(--md-red)">Terms</p>
      <h1>Short version: use them, they are free.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Last updated {updated}.</p>

      <h2>Using the tools</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on this site is free to use, including commercially, with no account and no
        attribution required. Check any domain you own, administer, or are otherwise allowed to
        look up. Every record we read is already public: anyone can query the same DNS from a
        terminal, and we do nothing you could not do yourself with <code>dig</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please do not automate the tools against the site. There are rate limits, and hitting them
        hard enough to affect other people will get the traffic blocked. If you want this
        programmatically, write to us and we will talk about it.
      </p>

      <h2>What we promise about accuracy</h2>
      <p>
        Every finding cites the specification it comes from, and the engines are unit tested against
        recorded DNS responses. We take being right seriously, because it is the only reason to
        trust anything else here.
      </p>
      <p>
        That said, DNS is a live system read from one place at one moment. Records propagate,
        resolvers disagree, and a result can be a few minutes behind reality. We show the disagreement
        rather than hide it when we see one, but you should confirm anything critical against your
        own DNS before you act.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do not promise</h2>
      <p>
        We do not guarantee that following a prescription will fix your delivery, and we are not
        liable for what happens when you change your DNS. Authentication records affect whether your
        mail arrives. Read each change, understand it, and if you are not sure, ask somebody who
        does this for a living. That is the whole reason the consultation form exists.
      </p>

      <h2>Consultation work</h2>
      <p>
        Asking for a consultation creates no obligation on either side. Paid work is agreed
        separately in writing before anything starts, with scope and price fixed up front.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If these terms change, the date at the top changes with them. Nothing here is retroactive.
      </p>

      <p>
        Questions: <a href="mailto:support@themaildoc.co">support@themaildoc.co</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ConsultLine />
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: var(--md-space-10) var(--md-space-12);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--md-text-2xl);
    margin-top: var(--md-space-3);
    letter-spacing: var(--md-tracking-tight);
  }

  .lead {
    color: var(--md-faint);
    font-size: var(--md-text-sm);
    margin-top: var(--md-space-2);
  }
</style>
